Looking at this problem, I need to count integers $1 \le n \le 70$ where $R(n) \mid Q(n)$.

**Setup.** For $n = \prod_{i=1}^k p_i^{e_i}$: $Q(n) = \prod p_i^{p_i}$ and $R(n) = \prod e_i^{e_i}$.

Since $Q(n) = \prod p_i^{p_i}$, the only primes dividing $Q(n)$ are the prime factors $p_i$ of $n$, each appearing with exponent $p_i$.

**Key conditions for $R(n) \mid Q(n)$:**
1. Every prime dividing any $e_i$ must be among the prime factors of $n$.
2. For each prime factor $p_j$ of $n$: $\sum_{i=1}^k e_i \cdot v_{p_j}(e_i) \le p_j$.

**Case 1: All $e_i = 1$ (squarefree $n$).** Then $R(n) = 1$, which divides everything. All squarefree numbers work.

Non-squarefree numbers $\le 70$ (divisible by $4, 9, 25,$ or $49$): by inclusion-exclusion, $17 + 7 + 2 + 1 - 1 = 26$. So **44 squarefree** numbers work (including $n=1$ and all 19 primes $\le 70$).

